\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><title>Close icon</title><path d=\"M11.782 4.032a.575.575 0 1 0-.813-.814L7.5 6.687 4.032 3.218a.575.575 0 0 0-.814.814L6.687 7.5l-3.469 3.468a.575.575 0 0 0 .814.814L7.5 8.313l3.469 3.469a.575.575 0 0 0 .813-.814L8.313 7.5l3.469-3.468Z\" fill=\"currentColor\" fill-rule=\"evenodd\" clip-rule=\"evenodd\"></path></svg><!----><span class=\"u-visually-hidden\">Close</span></button><!----><!----></dialog><!----></div><!----> <!--[-1--><!--]--> <!--[0--><div class=\"EventModules svelte-r64kdn\"><!--[--><!--[0--><!--[9--><div class=\"EventModuleRichText svelte-6ayzyh\"><!--[0--><h2 class=\"title f-heading-4 svelte-6ayzyh\">Description</h2><!--]--> <!--[-1--><!--]--> <div class=\"RichContent f-body-3 svelte-jgfn07\"><!--[0--><!----><p>In the bloodiest instalment of his hit Terror Talks series, dark historian Andrew Johnston (Alternative Ulstours) takes a startling new look at history’s most elusive serial killer – Jack the Ripper.</p><p>Belfast played a surprisingly intense role during 1888’s ‘Autumn of Terror’. Hysterical press coverage of the slayings in London led to horrific copycat attacks in Ulster, with vengeful Irish mobs targeting alleged ‘Rippers’.</p><p>Amidst this, a fugitive Englishman was arrested, charged and imprisoned in Belfast on suspicion of being ‘Jack’. The compelling case of William John Foster – dubbed Belfast’s ‘man with the knives’ – unfolded between the fourth and fifth canonical Ripper murders.</p><p>Did Belfast have the Whitechapel fiend in custody, and did local authorities let him slip through their fingers?</p><p>Andrew’s entertaining lecture will also shine a flashlight on a suspect with family ties to a venerated unionist politician, as well as exploring Belfast’s links to a potential Royal ‘Ripper’.</p><!----><!--]--></div><!----></div><!--]--><!--]--><!--]--></div><!--]--> <div class=\"HumanitixBannerLarge svelte-17kav4o\"><div class=\"logos svelte-17kav4o\"><span class=\"powered-by f-label-1 svelte-17kav4o\">Power